{"name":"Silver Sconce","hex":"#a19fa5","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"silver","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A lustrous, white, metallic element, atomic number 47, atomic weight 107.87, symbol Ag.","Coins made from silver or any similar white metal."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made from silver.","Made from another white metal."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/silver"},{"term":"sconce","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A fixture for a light, which holds it and provides a screen against wind or against a naked flame or lightbulb.\n\nA candlestick (holder for a candle, especially a circular tube, with a brim, into which a candle is inserted), either with a handle for carrying, or with a bracket for attaching to a wall.","A candlestick (holder for a candle, especially a circular tube, with a brim, into which a candle is inserted), either with a handle for carrying, or with a bracket for attaching to a wall."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A head or a skull.","A poll tax; a mulct or fine."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sconce"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sconce (light fixture)","blocks":[{"text":"A sconce or wall light is a decorative light fixture that is mounted to a wall. The sconce is a very old form of fixture, historically used with candles and oil lamps."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Sconce.jpg/330px-Sconce.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sconce_(light_fixture)","wikidata":"Q6653030"},{"title":"Silver","blocks":[{"text":"Silver is a chemical element; it has symbol Ag and atomic number 47. A soft, white, lustrous transition metal, it exhibits the highest electrical conductivity, thermal conductivity and reflectivity of any metal. Silver is found in the Earth's crust in the pure, free elemental form, as an alloy with gold (electrum) and other metals, and in minerals such as acanthite, argentite and chlorargyrite. Most silver is recovered as a byproduct of lead–zinc, copper and gold mining and refining."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Silver_crystal.jpg/330px-Silver_crystal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver","wikidata":"Q1090"},{"title":"Silver (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Silver or metallic gray is a color tone resembling gray that is a representation of the color of polished silver."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Clemens_Friedell_silver_centerpiece_bowl_top_view_-_HLABG.png/330px-Clemens_Friedell_silver_centerpiece_bowl_top_view_-_HLABG.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_(color)","wikidata":"Q317802"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Wandleuchter_Porzellan_KGM_W1966-43_img01.jpg/500px-Wandleuchter_Porzellan_KGM_W1966-43_img01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Wandleuchter_Porzellan_KGM_W1966-43_img01.jpg/1280px-Wandleuchter_Porzellan_KGM_W1966-43_img01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Wandleuchter_Porzellan_KGM_W1966-43_img01.jpg/500px-Wandleuchter_Porzellan_KGM_W1966-43_img01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Porcelain and ormolu sconce for candles, Royal Porcelain Factory, Berlin, 1770","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWandleuchter_Porzellan_KGM_W1966-43_img01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Sconce_on_the_Strozzi_palace%2C_Florence.jpg/500px-Sconce_on_the_Strozzi_palace%2C_Florence.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign